<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kotawaringin Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1615 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Kotawaringin</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Malay sultanate of south-western Borneo founded by a prince of Banjarmasin, long the main power on the Lamandau and Arut rivers.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Kotawaringin was founded in the early 17th century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pangeran-dipati-anta-kasuma">Pangeran Dipati Anta-Kasuma</a>, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Mustain Billah of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/banjar-sultanate">Banjar Sultanate</a>, who was given the western lands of Banjar to rule. Its court was first at Kotawaringin Lama on the Lamandau river and moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pangkalan-bun">Pangkalan Bun</a> in the early 19th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The sultans ruled a Malay Muslim riverine elite that collected tribute from inland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dayak-people">Dayak</a> communities, trading forest goods such as rattan, resin, gold and birds nests to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banjarmasin">Banjarmasin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. The court preserved Banjar customs and titles, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> spread among coastal settlers while most of the interior remained Dayak.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> brought Kotawaringin under contract in the 19th century as a self-governing domain of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>. The sultanate continued under colonial supervision and was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/indonesia">Republic of Indonesia</a> around 1948 to 1950. The royal palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pangkalan-bun">Pangkalan Bun</a> still stands.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kotawaringin-sultanate.jpg" alt="Kotawaringin Sultanate" width="320" height="180"><figcaption>Image: Germenfer · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Kingdom_of_Kotawaringin.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1615 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kotawaringin</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pangeran Dipati Anta-Kasuma</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malay (Banjar)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sultanate of Kotawaringin</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1782&amp;focus=kotawaringin-sultanate" aria-label="Kotawaringin Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7503 2200 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kotawaringin Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8181 2558h0M7959 2664h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7823 2456h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7823 2456h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Indonesia. <a href="/atlas?year=1782&amp;focus=kotawaringin-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
